[#:seon.task{:name "fs-read-ignores-limit-and-has-no-line-addressing"
             :plan "`my.fs/read` ignores :limit and has no line addressing (offset/count of lines), so agents read whole files. Done when :limit bounds the read and a line range is addressable, with a regression on a multi-line file. The open fault on `my.fs/read` (refused result missing `:my.fs/error-path`) is the same function. Read `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/repl-audit--read.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "archive-file-reads-have-no-explicit-source-world",
            :plan
            "source-display/printer-notes.md reports `my.fs/read` reads the running committed archive rather than the checkout, and reading 93 KB in 8 KB windows costs 1057 ms and 16600 armed calls. Make the source world explicit and bound work by requested content; measure the same read at the owner. Existing fs-read-ignores-limit-and-has-no-line-addressing covers limit/line addressing, not the archive-vs-tree ambiguity and per-window cost. Read `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/source-display/review-printer-codex-notes.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "the-write-examples-leave-files-in-the-checkout"
             :plan "The `my.fs/write!` and `my.edit` Examples write `example.txt` and `example.clj` relative to the checkout root, and an agent running them in SCI leaves them there untracked (found by running every docstring Example, 2026-10-14); a second run refuses on `:my.fs/expected-absence?`. Make each Example write under the checkout's gitignored tmp directory (or read-only), proven by running the Examples twice. Read `my.fs/write!`, `my.edit/form!`, `my.edit/exact!` and `my.edit/lines!`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "section-takes-a-trailing-anchor-keyword"
             :plan "Live run 2 (`docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-16/live-run-2.md`, chain 05db, twice): `(my.fs/section \"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-16/render-bench/report.md\" :anchor \"findings\")` was refused: `my.fs/section refused argument count: expected a declared argument count (1), got an argument count of 3`. Agents pass the heading as an option; `my.fs/section` takes it only after `#` in the one reference text. Decide at `my.fs/section` and `seon.ref/section` whether an `[:cat :seon.ref/reference-text [:= :anchor] :string]` arity is the verb's input, or the refusal should answer the one corrected form (`\"path#findings\"`); proof is a table row for the form above."}
 #:seon.task{:name "find-is-not-a-verb-the-agent-can-guess"
             :plan "Live run 2 (chain ce9d): `my.fs/find` was an unresolved name an agent invented; the file finder is `my.fs/glob`. Make the unresolved-name reply for a `my.*` namespace name the nearest declared verb (as `my.program/source` does for symbols), proven by the form `(my.fs/find {:my.fs/root \".\" :my.fs/pattern \"*.md\"})` answering `my.fs/glob`. Read `my.fs/glob` and `seon.sci.eval/install-row!`."}]
